-- chem/recoupling.cub — Wigner's 6j symbols, exactly, by Racah's formula. Changing the order in which three
-- spins are coupled is a 6j symbol, and every action of a holonomy at a vertex of a spin network, and every
-- operator q_IJK other than the one diagonalised in a given recoupling basis (Brunnemann–Thiemann, Class.
-- Quantum Grav. 23 (2006) 1289, section 3), is written with them. Racah (1942):
--   {j1 j2 j3; j4 j5 j6} = Δ(j1 j2 j3) Δ(j1 j5 j6) Δ(j4 j2 j6) Δ(j4 j5 j3)
--       Σ_t (−1)^t (t+1)! / [(t−a1)!(t−a2)!(t−a3)!(t−a4)!(b1−t)!(b2−t)!(b3−t)!],
--   a1 = j1+j2+j3, a2 = j1+j5+j6, a3 = j4+j2+j6, a4 = j4+j5+j3,
--   b1 = j1+j2+j4+j5, b2 = j2+j3+j5+j6, b3 = j3+j1+j6+j4,
--   Δ(a b c)² = (a+b−c)!(a−b+c)!(−a+b+c)!/(a+b+c+1)!.
-- So a 6j symbol is a sign and the square root of a rational: the sum is a rational, the Δs square roots of
-- rationals. Spins are doubled, as in chem/spin.cub; a symbol is kept as its sign and its square.
import "volume.cub"

